  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Aquaculture farmers&apos; needs assessment in the Mekong Delta, Vietnam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vietnam is recognised as the largest exporter of shrimp and aquaculture products to the Netherlands. The signing of the EVFTA between the European Union and Vietnam has opened up opportunities for increased trade and cooperation between the two countries. However, Vietnam faces significant challenges related to diseases and environmental pressures in its aquaculture industry. Dutch expertise in farming practices can play a crucial role in addressing these challenges, improving production methods, and reducing environmental impacts.The Mekong Delta region in Vietnam stands out as the most productive area for brackish and freshwater aquaculture and fisheries due to its favorable environmental conditions, vast water surface area, and abundant fisheries resources. It is also a priority area for the Netherlands in Vietnam.Despite these advantages, aquaculture producers, both individual and cooperative in the Mekong Delta region, encounter several issues:&#x2022;	Fragmented production with low quality and productivity.&#x2022;	Limited access to quality inputs such as seeds, feed, probiotics, and services.&#x2022;	Insufficient access to innovative and affordable technologies.&#x2022;	Lack of access to financial services.&#x2022;	Limited skills and capacity to adopt good aquaculture practices, ensure production safety, and cope with climate change, disease outbreaks, environmental concerns, and higher import requirements.&#x2022;	Modest engagement of small farmers in sustainable supply chains with lead firms.The objective of the project is to provide a comprehensive understanding of the needs and challenges faced by aquaculture farmers, explore the factors influencing their decision-making processes, and identify areas requiring intervention. The project aims to leverage Dutch expertise and innovations to support aquaculture farmers effectively.Specifically, the project will target farmers in key provinces of aquaculture production in the Mekong Delta region in Vietnam, where the insights gained will contribute to addressing the identified needs and facilitating the implementation of relevant interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
